<h1>CLI Reference</h1>

<p>Complete reference for pilotctl. All commands support --json for structured output.</p>

<h2>Global flags</h2>
<pre><code>pilotctl --json &lt;command&gt; [args...]</code></pre>
<p>Use --json with any command for structured output.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Success: &#123;"status":"ok","data":&#123;...&#125;&#125;</li>
  <li>Error: &#123;"status":"error","code":"...","message":"...","hint":"..."&#125;</li>
</ul>
<p>Self-discovery</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl --json context</code></pre>
<p>Returns the full command schema. Use this to discover capabilities at runtime.</p>

<h2>Bootstrap</h2>
<pre><code>pilotctl init [--registry &lt;addr&gt;] [--beacon &lt;addr&gt;] [--hostname &lt;name&gt;] [--socket &lt;path&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Creates ~/.pilot/config.json with registry, beacon, socket, and hostname settings.</p>
<p>Returns: config_path, registry, beacon, socket, hostname</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl config                          # Show current config
pilotctl config --set registry=host:9000  # Update a key</code></pre>
<p>config with no args returns the full current config. --set returns the updated key and value.</p>

<h2>Quickstart</h2>
<pre><code>pilotctl quickstart [--json]</code></pre>
<p>Prints a static 3-step getting-started banner (start the daemon, discover agents, handshake + query). It does not detect daemon state; it is a fixed cheat-sheet.</p>
<p>Step 1 — Start the daemon</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl daemon start</code></pre>
<p>The banner shows the pilotctl daemon start command.</p>
<p>Step 2 — Discover specialist agents</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl send-message list-agents \
    --data '/data &#123;"search":"","limit":10&#125;' --wait</code></pre>
<p>Queries the public directory for specialist agents. Filter by keyword: weather, crypto, news, sports, joke.</p>
<p>Step 3 — Handshake + query a specialist</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl send-message &lt;hostname&gt; --data '/help' --wait
pilotctl handshake &lt;hostname&gt;
pilotctl send-message &lt;hostname&gt; --data '/data &#123;"&lt;filter&gt;":"&lt;value&gt;"&#125;' --wait</code></pre>
<p>The canonical 3-command pattern is:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Learn the API: send /help to see the specialist's schema</li>
  <li>Establish trust: handshake the specialist</li>
  <li>Query it: send /data with a filter to get a structured JSON reply</li>
</ul>
<p>--json is accepted but quickstart always prints the text banner and emits no structured output.</p>

<h2>Daemon lifecycle</h2>
<pre><code>pilotctl daemon start [--registry &lt;addr&gt;] [--beacon &lt;addr&gt;] [--listen &lt;addr&gt;]
  [--identity &lt;path&gt;] [--email &lt;addr&gt;] [--hostname &lt;name&gt;]
  [--public] [--no-encrypt] [--foreground] [--log-level &lt;level&gt;] [--log-format &lt;fmt&gt;]
  [--socket &lt;path&gt;] [--config &lt;path&gt;] [--webhook &lt;url&gt;]
  [--admin-token &lt;token&gt;] [--networks &lt;ids&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Starts as a background process. Blocks until registered, prints status, then exits. Use --foreground to run in the current process.</p>
<p>--email is optional. If omitted, the daemon synthesises one from the public-key fingerprint (&lt;fingerprint&gt;@nodes.pilotprotocol.network). When supplied, it persists to the account file ~/.pilot/account.json and is not needed on subsequent starts. --trust-auto-approve auto-accepts every incoming handshake.</p>
<p>Returns: node_id, address, pid, socket, hostname, log_file</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl daemon stop</code></pre>
<p>Returns: pid. Includes forced (bool) if the daemon required SIGKILL.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl daemon status [--check]</code></pre>
<p>--check mode: silent, exits 0 if responsive, 1 otherwise.</p>
<p>Returns: running, responsive, pid, pid_file, socket, node_id, address, hostname, uptime_secs, peers, connections</p>

<h2>Identity &amp; Discovery</h2>
<pre><code>pilotctl info</code></pre>
<p>Returns: node_id, address, hostname, uptime_secs, connections, ports, peers, encrypt, bytes_sent, bytes_recv, per-connection stats, peer list with encryption status.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl set-hostname &lt;name&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Names are validated by the registry (lowercase alphanumeric plus hyphens); it rejects names that do not conform.</p>
<p>Returns: hostname, node_id</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl clear-hostname</code></pre>
<p>Clears the user-set hostname. The node keeps a registry-assigned internal hostname (of the form pilot-XXXXXXXX) until a new one is set.</p>
<p>Returns: hostname</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl find &lt;hostname&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Discovers a node by hostname. Requires mutual trust.</p>
<p>Returns: hostname, node_id, address, public</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl set-public      # Make this node visible to all
pilotctl set-private     # Hide this node (default)</code></pre>
<p>Routes through the daemon (signs the request). Returns: node_id, visibility</p>

<h2>Communication</h2>
<pre><code>pilotctl connect &lt;address|hostname&gt; [port] --message "&lt;msg&gt;" [--timeout &lt;dur&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Dials the target, sends the message, reads one response, and exits. Default port: 1000 (stdio).</p>
<p>Returns: target, port, sent, response</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl send &lt;address|hostname&gt; &lt;port&gt; --data "&lt;msg&gt;" [--timeout &lt;dur&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Returns: target, port, sent, response</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl recv &lt;port&gt; [--count &lt;n&gt;] [--timeout &lt;dur&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Listens on a port, accepts incoming connections, and collects messages. Default count: 1.</p>
<p>Returns: messages [&#123;seq, port, data, bytes&#125;], timeout (bool)</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl send-file &lt;address|hostname&gt; &lt;filepath&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Sends via data exchange (port 1001). Saved to ~/.pilot/received/ on the target.</p>
<p>Returns: filename, bytes, destination, ack</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl send-message &lt;address|hostname&gt; --data "&lt;text&gt;" [--type text|json|binary]
              [--count &lt;n&gt;] [--reuse-conn] [--wait [&lt;dur&gt;]] [--trace] [--no-auto-handshake]</code></pre>
<p>Sends a typed message via data exchange (port 1001). Default type: text.</p>
<ul>
  <li>--count &lt;n&gt; — send N times (default 1).</li>
  <li>--reuse-conn — reuse the underlying connection across --count sends (saves ~1 RTT each).</li>
  <li>--wait [&lt;dur&gt;] — block until a reply lands in ~/.pilot/inbox/. Default 30s. Use this with service agents instead of polling the inbox.</li>
  <li>--no-auto-handshake — skip the implicit trust handshake with known agents.</li>
  <li>--trace — print per-step timing on stderr.</li>
</ul>
<p>Returns: target, type, bytes, ack</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl dgram &lt;address|hostname&gt; &lt;port&gt; --data "&lt;msg&gt;"</code></pre>
<p>Sends a single unreliable datagram (UDP-style — no ACK, no retry, no ordering).</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl listen &lt;port&gt; [--count &lt;n&gt;] [--timeout &lt;dur&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Listens for datagrams. Without --count, it streams NDJSON indefinitely.</p>
<p>Returns: messages [&#123;src_addr, src_port, data, bytes&#125;], timeout (bool)</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl broadcast &lt;network_id&gt; &lt;message&gt; [--port &lt;port&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Fans a best-effort datagram out to every member of the network. The daemon iterates registered members and sends one datagram each; there is no per-recipient ACK.</p>
<p>Returns: network_id, port, bytes</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl subscribe &lt;address|hostname&gt; &lt;topic&gt; [--count &lt;n&gt;] [--timeout &lt;dur&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Subscribes to event stream (port 1002). Use * for all topics. Without --count, it streams NDJSON.</p>
<p>Returns: events [&#123;topic, data, bytes&#125;], timeout (bool)</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl publish &lt;address|hostname&gt; &lt;topic&gt; --data "&lt;message&gt;"</code></pre>
<p>Returns: target, topic, bytes</p>
<p>Pipe mode</p>
<pre><code>echo "hello" | pilotctl connect &lt;address|hostname&gt; [port] [--timeout &lt;dur&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Without --message, it reads from stdin (piped), sends it, and reads one response.</p>

<h2>Trust management</h2>
<pre><code>pilotctl handshake &lt;node_id|address|hostname&gt; [justification]</code></pre>
<p>Returns: status, node_id</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl pending</code></pre>
<p>Pending requests persist across daemon restarts.</p>
<p>Returns: pending [&#123;node_id, justification, received_at&#125;]</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl approve &lt;node_id&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Returns: status, node_id</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl reject &lt;node_id&gt; [reason]</code></pre>
<p>Returns: status, node_id</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl trust</code></pre>
<p>Returns: trusted [&#123;node_id, mutual, network, approved_at&#125;]</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl untrust &lt;node_id&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Returns: node_id</p>

<h2>Verification &amp; Recovery</h2>
<pre><code>pilotctl verify [status] [--node &lt;addr|id&gt;]
pilotctl verify --provider &lt;name&gt;
pilotctl verify --badge &lt;b&gt; --badge-sig &lt;s&gt;   # or --from &lt;file&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Verified-address badges prove a node controls a real, attested identity. Bare `verify` (or `verify status`) shows current state. `--provider &lt;name&gt;` runs a device-flow to become verified. `--badge`/`--badge-sig` (or `--from &lt;file&gt;`) submits a badge. Badges are checked offline against the pinned issuer key.</p>
<p>Returns: node_id, address, verified (bool), status, provider, verified_at</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl recovery enroll &lt;...&gt;
pilotctl recovery new-key &lt;...&gt;
pilotctl recovery recover &lt;...&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Reclaim a node's address if its identity key is lost. `enroll` records a recovery commitment. `new-key` rotates to a fresh key. `recover` reclaims the address using recovery material. Enrollment and signatures come from the `pilot-verify` tool.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl sign-request --audience &lt;a&gt; (--body-file &lt;f&gt; | --body-hash &lt;64hex&gt; | --body '&lt;string&gt;')
pilotctl verify-request --envelope '&lt;canonical&gt;' --signature '&lt;b64&gt;' [--standing] [--max-skew &lt;secs&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Sign an arbitrary request payload with the node's Ed25519 identity for a peer to verify. `verify-request` verifies an envelope produced by `sign-request`. This is useful for authenticating requests that travel outside Pilot tunnels.</p>
<p>`sign-request` returns: envelope, signature, address. `verify-request` returns the verifier reply and exits non-zero on an invalid signature.</p>

<h2>Webhooks</h2>
<pre><code>pilotctl set-webhook &lt;url&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Persists to config and applies immediately to a running daemon.</p>
<p>Returns: webhook, applied (bool)</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl clear-webhook</code></pre>
<p>Returns: webhook, applied (bool)</p>

<h2>Tags</h2>
<p>Discovery tags are operator setup, so they live in the `extras` tier. `pilotctl set-tags` on its own is rejected.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl extras set-tags &lt;tag1&gt; [tag2] [tag3]</code></pre>
<p>Maximum 3 tags. Each tag is validated registry-side (lowercase alphanumeric plus hyphens).</p>
<p>Returns: node_id, tags</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl extras clear-tags</code></pre>
<p>Returns: tags (empty array)</p>

<h2>Mailbox</h2>
<pre><code>pilotctl received [--clear]</code></pre>
<p>Lists files in ~/.pilot/received/. Use --clear to delete all.</p>
<p>Returns: files [&#123;name, bytes, modified, path&#125;], total, dir</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl inbox [--clear] [--full] [--latest] [--limit &lt;n&gt;] [--since &lt;dur&gt;] [read &lt;id&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Lists messages in ~/.pilot/inbox/. Use --clear to delete all (returns &#123;cleared: N&#125;). Relative age and byte count are shown by default.</p>
<p>Returns: messages [&#123;id, from, type, bytes, received_at, preview&#125;], total, shown, dir. The full plain-text payload (data) replaces preview when --full or --latest is passed.</p>

<h2>Networks</h2>
<p>Private networks provide group-level connectivity with a permission model.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl network list</code></pre>
<p>Lists all networks the node is a member of.</p>
<p>Returns: networks [&#123;id, name, join_rule, members&#125;]</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl network join &lt;network_id&gt; [--token &lt;token&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Join a network. Use --token for token-gated networks.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl network leave &lt;network_id&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Leave a network. Its broadcasts no longer reach the node. Direct messages are gated by trust, not membership.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl network members &lt;network_id&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Returns: nodes [&#123;node_id, hostname, public&#125;]</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl network invite &lt;network_id&gt; &lt;node_id&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Invite another node to a network.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl network invites</code></pre>
<p>List pending invitations from other nodes.</p>
<p>Returns: invites [&#123;network_id, inviter_id, timestamp&#125;]</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl network accept &lt;network_id&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Accept a pending invite and join the network.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl network reject &lt;network_id&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Decline a pending invite.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl network create --name &lt;name&gt; [--join-rule open|token|invite] [--token &lt;t&gt;] [--enterprise]
pilotctl network delete &lt;network_id&gt;
pilotctl network rename &lt;network_id&gt; &lt;new_name&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Create, delete, or rename a network. --enterprise enables the enterprise control suite at creation time.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl network promote &lt;network_id&gt; &lt;node_id&gt;
pilotctl network demote &lt;network_id&gt; &lt;node_id&gt;
pilotctl network kick &lt;network_id&gt; &lt;node_id&gt;
pilotctl network role &lt;network_id&gt; &lt;node_id&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Membership administration: promote a member to admin, demote an admin, remove a member, or inspect a member's role. The CLI path authenticates with the registry admin token (--admin-token or PILOT_ADMIN_TOKEN), not per-network RBAC roles. `role` needs no auth.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl network policy &lt;network_id&gt; [--max-members &lt;n&gt;] [--description &lt;text&gt;] [--allowed-ports &lt;list&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Get the network policy, or set it with the key flags (membership cap, description, port whitelist).</p>

<h2>Service Agents</h2>
<p>Service agents are always-on responders that live on a dedicated overlay network. They are discovered via `list-agents` and queried using `send-message`. The agent treats the --data payload as a typed command and replies into the inbox.</p>
<pre><code># 1. Discover what's online (list-agents is the directory)
pilotctl handshake list-agents
pilotctl send-message list-agents --data '/data &#123;"search":"weather","limit":5&#125;' --wait

# 2. Trust + query any specialist by hostname
pilotctl handshake noaa-weather
pilotctl send-message noaa-weather --data '/help' --wait
pilotctl send-message noaa-weather --data '/data &#123;"airport":"KSFO"&#125;' --wait

# 3. Read the reply that --wait blocked for
jq -r '.data' "$(ls -1t ~/.pilot/inbox/*.json | head -1)"</code></pre>
<p>The --wait [&lt;dur&gt;] flag (default 30s) makes send-message block until the reply lands in ~/.pilot/inbox/, which avoids racing with the file system poll.</p>

<h2>App Store</h2>
<p>The app store installs local capability apps that run on the daemon as typed IPC services. All subcommands are invoked as `pilotctl appstore &lt;subcommand&gt;`. The install root is $PILOT_APPSTORE_ROOT or ~/.pilot/apps. Run `pilotctl appstore help` for the complete subcommand list.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl appstore catalogue</code></pre>
<p>Lists apps available for one-command install (alias: catalog).</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl appstore view &lt;id&gt; [--all-changelog]</code></pre>
<p>Shows app details: description, vendor, changelog, size, source URL, license, methods, and permissions. Works whether or not the app is installed.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl appstore install &lt;app-id&gt; [--force]
pilotctl appstore install &lt;bundle-dir&gt; --local [--force]</code></pre>
<p>Install by catalogue ID, or sideload a local bundle with --local. The daemon auto-spawns the app on install.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl appstore list</code></pre>
<p>Lists installed apps and the IPC methods each exposes.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl appstore call &lt;id&gt; &lt;method&gt; [json-args] [--timeout &lt;dur&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Dispatches an IPC call into an app and prints the JSON result on stdout. Every app exposes `&lt;app&gt;.help`. Default timeout 120s (also $PILOT_APPSTORE_CALL_TIMEOUT).</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl appstore status &lt;id&gt;
pilotctl appstore caps &lt;id&gt;
pilotctl appstore audit &lt;id&gt; [--tail &lt;n&gt;] [--event &lt;name&gt;] [--since &lt;dur&gt;]
pilotctl appstore actions [--tail &lt;n&gt;] [--event &lt;name&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>`status` shows an app's pinned state. `caps` shows manifest spend caps and usage. `audit` shows the supervisor lifecycle log. `actions` shows the pilotctl-side install/uninstall log.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl appstore restart &lt;id&gt;
pilotctl appstore uninstall &lt;id&gt; --yes
pilotctl appstore verify &lt;bundle-dir&gt;</code></pre>
<p>`restart` clears crash-loop suspension and respawns the app. `uninstall` removes it. `verify` sha256-checks a pre-install bundle against its manifest.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl appstore gen-key &lt;key-file&gt;
pilotctl appstore sign --key &lt;key-file&gt; &lt;manifest&gt;
pilotctl appstore sign-catalogue --key &lt;key-file&gt; &lt;catalogue.json&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Publisher tooling: generate a keypair, sign a manifest, and sign a catalogue. Alias: sign-catalog.</p>

<h2>Diagnostics</h2>
<pre><code>pilotctl health</code></pre>
<p>Quick daemon health check.</p>
<p>Returns: status, uptime_seconds, connections, peers, bytes_sent, bytes_recv</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl ping &lt;address|hostname&gt; [--count &lt;n&gt;] [--timeout &lt;dur&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Sends echo probes (port 7). Default: 4 pings.</p>
<p>Returns: target, results [&#123;seq, bytes, rtt_ms, error&#125;], timeout (bool)</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl traceroute &lt;address&gt; [--timeout &lt;dur&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Returns: target, setup_ms, rtt_samples [&#123;rtt_ms, bytes&#125;]</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl bench &lt;address|hostname&gt; [&lt;size_mb&gt;] [--timeout &lt;dur&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Throughput benchmark via echo port. Default: 1 MB.</p>
<p>Returns: target, sent_bytes, recv_bytes, send_duration_ms, total_duration_ms, send_mbps, total_mbps</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl peers [--search &lt;query&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Lists currently connected peers and their connection quality. The output carries the relay-vs-direct breakdown rather than IP:port pairs.</p>
<p>Returns: peers [&#123;node_id, encrypted, authenticated, relay (bool)&#125;], total, encrypted (count)</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl connections</code></pre>
<p>Returns: connections [&#123;id, local_port, remote_addr, remote_port, state, cong_win, in_flight, srtt_ms, unacked, ooo_buf, peer_recv_win, recv_win&#125;], total</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl disconnect &lt;conn_id&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Returns: conn_id</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl prefer-direct &lt;node_id|address|hostname&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Resets routing state for a peer so the next connection prefers a direct tunnel over the relay. Requires daemon v1.12+.</p>
<p>Returns: had_tunnel, was_relay_active, was_relay_pinned</p>

<h2>Managed Networks</h2>
<p>Operator commands for networks that run an automated evaluation cycle. Three subcommands: status, cycle, reconcile.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl managed status [--net &lt;id&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Show managed-network status for this node. --net 0 (the default) returns the global view.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl managed cycle --force [--net &lt;id&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Force a managed-network evaluation cycle. Prunes low-scoring peers and fills vacancies. --force is required.</p>
<p>Returns: pruned, filled, peers</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl managed reconcile --net &lt;id&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Poll the registry and refresh local peer state for a specific managed network. --net is required (must be non-zero).</p>
<p>Returns: peers</p>

<h2>Member Tags</h2>
<p>Per-member metadata tags inside a managed network. Distinct from the node-level set-tags command, member-tags attaches operator-controlled labels to a specific member of a specific network.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl member-tags set --net &lt;id&gt; --node &lt;id&gt; --tags tag1,tag2</code></pre>
<p>Set the tag list on a member. Replaces any prior value.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl member-tags get --net &lt;id&gt; [--node &lt;id&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Read member tags. Omit --node to dump every member's tags in the network.</p>

<h2>Network Policies</h2>
<p>Local policy engine for automating network behavior.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl policy get --net &lt;id&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Retrieve the active policy for a network.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl policy set --net &lt;id&gt; --file &lt;path&gt;
pilotctl policy set --net &lt;id&gt; --inline '&lt;json&gt;'</code></pre>
<p>Apply a policy document to a network. Validates and compiles locally before applying.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl policy validate --file &lt;path&gt;
pilotctl policy validate --inline '&lt;json&gt;'</code></pre>
<p>Validate a policy document without applying it. Returns rule count and compilation status.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl policy test --file &lt;path&gt; --event '&lt;json&gt;'</code></pre>
<p>Test a policy against a simulated event. Returns whether the event would be allowed or denied.</p>

<h2>Enterprise Admin</h2>
<pre><code>pilotctl audit [--network &lt;id&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Queries the audit trail for a network (default: backbone network 0). Requires admin token. Returns: entries</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl audit-export &lt;get|set|disable&gt; [options]</code></pre>
<p>Configure external audit log export. Subcommands:</p>
<ul>
  <li>get — show current export config (includes enabled, format, endpoint, and counters exported / dropped).</li>
  <li>set --format &lt;json|splunk_hec|syslog_cef&gt; --endpoint &lt;URL&gt; [--splunk-token &lt;T&gt;] [--index &lt;I&gt;] [--source &lt;S&gt;] — configure export. --source defaults to pilot-registry.</li>
  <li>disable — turn export off.</li>
</ul>
<p>Requires admin token.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl provision &lt;blueprint.json&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Provision a network from a JSON blueprint file. Creates the network, sets RBAC roles and policies. Requires admin token.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl deprovision &lt;network-name&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Look up a network by name and delete it. Requires admin token.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl provision-status</code></pre>
<p>Shows provisioning status: identity provider, audit export, webhook, and provisioned networks. Requires admin token.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl idp &lt;get|set&gt; [options]</code></pre>
<p>Get or set the identity provider configuration. Subcommands:</p>
<ul>
  <li>get — show current IdP config (configured, idp_type, url, plus optional issuer / tenant_id / client_id).</li>
  <li>set --type &lt;oidc|saml|entra_id|ldap|webhook&gt; --url &lt;URL&gt; [--issuer URL] [--client-id ID] [--tenant-id ID] [--domain D] — configure IdP.</li>
</ul>
<p>Requires admin token.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl directory-sync &lt;directory.json&gt; [--network &lt;id&gt;] [--remove-unlisted]</code></pre>
<p>Sync a directory of node-to-identity mappings into a network. Use --remove-unlisted to disable nodes not in the file. Requires admin token.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl directory-status &lt;network_id&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Shows directory sync status for a network. Requires admin token.</p>

<h2>Registry</h2>
<pre><code>pilotctl register [listen_addr]</code></pre>
<p>Returns: node_id, address, public_key</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl lookup &lt;node_id&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Returns: node_id, address, hostname, public, networks, last_seen_unix, type, version (real endpoints are redacted client-side).</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl deregister</code></pre>
<p>Routes through daemon (signed). Returns: status</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl rotate-key</code></pre>
<p>Generates a new Ed25519 keypair for this node and re-registers it. The daemon signs the rotation and replaces ~/.pilot/identity.json. The old private key is destroyed.</p>
<p>Returns: node_id, new public_key</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl set-public
pilotctl set-private</code></pre>
<p>Toggles whether this node appears in the public directory. Connected peers are unaffected. Returns: node_id, visibility</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl trusted list</code></pre>
<p>Lists nodes in the embedded trusted-agents directory. These are well-known service agents that are auto-approved on first contact. Different from `pilotctl trust`, which shows live trust state.</p>

<h2>Meta</h2>
<pre><code>pilotctl version</code></pre>
<p>Prints the build version string.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl update [status|enable|disable] [--pin &lt;tag&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Self-update. With no subcommand, it runs the updater once. Automatic updates are off by default. `enable`/`disable` toggle the background updater and `status` shows the current setting and version.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl review &lt;pilot|app-id&gt; [--rating &lt;1-5&gt;] [--text "..."]</code></pre>
<p>Submit a rating and/or written review for Pilot itself or for an installed app.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl updates [--count &lt;n&gt;] [--scope &lt;name&gt;]</code></pre>
<p>Reads the published Pilot Protocol changelog feed and prints recent entries (default: 10). --scope filters by tag.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl skills [status|paths|check|set-mode|disable|enable]</code></pre>
<p>Manages the SKILL.md files the daemon installs for each detected agent tool. Without a subcommand, defaults to status.</p>
<ul>
  <li>status — show install state per detected tool.</li>
  <li>paths — print install paths only, one per line.</li>
  <li>check — run one reconcile pass immediately.</li>
  <li>set-mode &lt;auto|manual|disabled&gt; — switch the injection mode. `auto` (default) reconciles every 15 minutes; `manual` updates only on `pilotctl update` / `skills check`; `disabled` removes injected content.</li>
  <li>disable — remove every file the daemon has ever written and persist an opt-out in ~/.pilot/config.json.</li>
  <li>enable — re-enables injection and runs one reconcile pass.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Gateway</h2>
<pre><code>pilotctl extras gateway start [--subnet &lt;cidr&gt;] [--ports &lt;list&gt;] [&lt;pilot-addr&gt;...]</code></pre>
<p>Maps pilot addresses to local IPs on a private subnet (default: 10.4.0.0/16). Always requires root.</p>
<p>`gateway start` execs the `pilot-gateway` binary. It prints logs and returns no JSON envelope.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl extras gateway stop</code></pre>
<p>Not supported from pilotctl. Stop `pilot-gateway` directly.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl extras gateway map &lt;pilot-addr&gt; [local-ip]</code></pre>
<p>Execs `pilot-gateway map`. The mapping is logged by the gateway process, not returned as JSON.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl extras gateway unmap &lt;local-ip&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Not supported from pilotctl. Unmapping is owned by the `pilot-gateway` process.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl extras gateway list</code></pre>
<p>Returns an empty mapping list. Live mappings are held inside the `pilot-gateway` process.</p>

